# LDBC SNB -- no_type_index schema (--schema no_type_index): the canonical design MINUS the
# per-type membership indexes. A WRITE-MOTIVATED design, and the first one chosen from the
# two-currency scoreboard rather than from the read side.
#
# WHY THIS DESIGN EXISTS. On dgraph the type-enumeration index is ONE posting per label holding
# every uid of that label, so inserting a vertex appends to it -- and at rollup that posting is
# rewritten WHOLE. It is by far the largest single write cost measured: of the 2.85 MB an IU6
# add-post writes at k=1, 2.17 MB is ('dgraph.type','I','Post') alone. Dropping
# it should be the biggest available write win.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS ON READS. dgraph loses the index-once path for a type filter: filter_via falls back
# to the per-vid probe (read_filter_by_label_probe), so the type filter goes from 1 SEEK to N SEEK (ADR 0001). It also
# loses type ENUMERATION entirely -- read_vertices_by_label refuses on this design rather than
# returning an empty list -- so this design is only valid for plans that enter by a value index,
# which is why it derives from canonical (value + date indexes present) and NOT from label_only.
#
# The per-vertex ("dgraph.type","D",vid) record is UNCONDITIONAL and still written: it is the
# structural type record the probe path reads. Only the INDEX is dropped.
#
# nebula and arango are largely unaffected, because of how they store types: nebula's empty tag
# index keys on whether a label has a required PROPERTY index (not on this declaration) and arango folds the type
# into the collection-qualified id. That asymmetry is the point -- this is a predicate-store knob.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- vertices ----
vertex Person prefix=person
    firstName    str
    lastName     str
    gender       str
    birthday     date
    creationDate date
    locationIP   str
    browserUsed  str
    email        str multi
    language     str multi

# Post & Comment share one id space (group=Message -> the msg_ vid prefix); the concrete
# label still rides in storage, so a query recovers Post-vs-Comment, never from the vid.
vertex Post prefix=msg group=Message
    imageFile    str nullable
    creationDate date
    locationIP   str
    browserUsed  str
    language     str nullable
    content      str nullable
    length       int

vertex Comment prefix=msg group=Message
    creationDate date
    locationIP   str
    browserUsed  str
    content      str
    length       int

vertex Forum prefix=forum
    title        str
    creationDate date

vertex Organisation prefix=org
    type str
    name str
    url  str

vertex Place prefix=place
    name str
    url  str
    type str

vertex Tag prefix=tag
    name str
    url  str

vertex TagClass prefix=tagclass
    name str
    url  str

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- edges ----
# `reverse` = the workload traverses it backward (dgraph @reverse, per-predicate). One etype
# can span many endpoint pairs. Edge props are read by name from the CSV.
edge knows reverse
    Person->Person
    creationDate date

edge likes reverse
    Person->Post Person->Comment
    creationDate date

edge hasCreator reverse
    Post->Person Comment->Person

edge hasTag reverse
    Post->Tag Comment->Tag Forum->Tag

edge hasType reverse
    Tag->TagClass

edge isLocatedIn reverse
    Person->Place Post->Place Comment->Place Organisation->Place

edge replyOf reverse
    Comment->Post Comment->Comment

edge containerOf reverse
    Forum->Post

edge hasMember reverse
    Forum->Person
    joinDate date

edge hasModerator reverse
    Forum->Person

edge isPartOf reverse
    Place->Place

edge isSubclassOf reverse
    TagClass->TagClass

# forward-only (no @reverse)
edge hasInterest
    Person->Tag

edge studyAt
    Person->Organisation
    classYear int

edge workAt
    Person->Organisation
    workFrom int

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- indexes ----
# `index Label.prop` = a property index; the kind is inferred from the column type
# (string -> equality, date/number -> sorted). `index Label` (no prop) = a per-type
# membership (type-enumeration) index.
index Person.firstName
index Tag.name
index TagClass.name
index Place.name
index Person.creationDate
index Post.creationDate
index Comment.creationDate

# NO per-type membership index -- that is the whole point of this design. The canonical note about
# why it is a real structure still applies; here we price its ABSENCE. (Original note:
# This is a REAL structure of the canonical design,
# not an opt-in: dgraph mandates it (dgraph.type -- opaque uids can't be typed without it), and
# nebula/arango materialize it faithfully. The SCHEMA declares that the structure exists; whether
# a given FORMAT is "fair" or "faithful" is a separate axis (how it prices that structure), so a
# per-type index in canonical is symmetric across formats, not an advantage for any one. It is the
# enumeration structure read_vertices_by_label scans and the "index" path read_filter_by_label_index takes.
